      ],
      "source_raw_fragment": "<p id=\"A2.SS2.p1.1\" class=\"ltx_p\">We begin with the reflection operator <math id=\"A2.SS2.p1.m1\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathsf{U}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>𝖴</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathsf{U}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let\n<math id=\"A2.SS2.p1.m2\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"f\\in\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}}\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℌ</mi><mi>𝖯</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\in\\mathfrak{H}_{\\mathsf{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so that\n<math id=\"A2.SS2.p1.m3\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"f(x,y)=f(y)\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x,y)=f(y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Although <math id=\"A2.SS2.p1.m4\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"f\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math> initially depends only on the auxiliary\ncoordinate <math id=\"A2.SS2.p1.m5\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"y\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y</annotation></semantics></math>, reflection gives <math id=\"A2.SS2.p1.m6\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"(\\mathsf{U}f)(x,y)=f(2x-y)\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝖴</mi><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\mathsf{U}f)(x,y)=f(2x-y)</annotation></semantics></math>, which generally depends on both coordinates. Its macroscopic and microscopic\ncomponents are <math id=\"A2.SS2.p1.m7\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathsf{U}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}f\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mi>𝖯𝖯</mi></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathsf{U}_{\\mathsf{P}\\mathsf{P}}f</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id=\"A2.SS2.p1.m8\" class=\"ltx_Math\" alttext=\"\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}f\" display=\"inline\" intent=\":literal\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝖴</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>𝖯</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathsf{U}_{\\perp\\mathsf{P}}f</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The following\nprobabilistic representation identifies the squared norm of the microscopic\ncomponent created by reflection with an expected conditional variance, thereby\nenabling quantitative estimate by the conditional Poincaré estimates used in\nAppendix <a href=\"#A3\" title=\"Appendix C Macroscopic coercivity and the half-turn bound ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler\" class=\"ltx_ref\"><span class=\"ltx_text ltx_ref_tag\">C</span></a>.</p>"
    },
